Content-Transfer-Encoding: quoted-printable
Content-Type: text/plain;
	charset=us-ascii

Hi Rob,

This response misses the broader issue. As Professor Bernstein noted =
during the SSH WG discussions, current practices by WG Chairs are =
undermining active participation within the Working Group, specifically =
highlighting the points outlined as "2. Chairs sabotaging participation" =
and "3. Chairs sabotaging resolution of technical disputes" [1].
This observation has been confirmed through my personal experience in =
both the TLS and SSH Working Groups.

Kind regards,

Ken Kubota

____________________________________________________

Ken Kubota
https://doi.org/10.4444/100


[1] =
https://mailarchive.ietf.org/arch/msg/ssh/KwzbhHP-6cK4VSWT04mNv_cWyFI/



> Am 15.10.2026 um 12:57 schrieb Rob Wilton (rwilton) =
<rwilton=3D40cisco.com@dmarc.ietf.org>:
>=20
> Hi Waqas,
>=20
> I don't follow SEAT, and don't know this document or discussion, but I =
do understand the IETF process rules pretty well.
>=20
> At that first data tracker link you provided you will see:
>=20
> "This document is an Internet-Draft (I-D). Anyone may submit an I-D to =
the IETF. This I-D is not endorsed by the IETF and has no formal =
standing in the IETF standards process =
<https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/rfc2026/>."
>=20
> Unless the SEAT WG has adopted the document (and hence the name of the =
draft changes to draft-ietf-seat-early-attestation, then this is NOT an =
IETF document and hence it is incorrect to say that the SEAT WG is =
"working on it".  The only status that this draft has is that it is an =
idea from a set of authors which they would like the SEAT WG to =
consider.  Anyone can submit a draft for consideration by the WG.  =
Perhaps the authors will consider your patch, perhaps they won't.  It is =
up to them since they are currently solely responsible for the document, =
not the SEAT WG, nor the IETF.
>=20
> Also worth seeing =
https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-wkumari-not-a-draft/.
>=20
> Kind regards,
> Rob
>=20
>=20
> From: waqas.nawaz@vision4d.ai <waqas.nawaz@vision4d.ai>
> Date: Thursday, 15 October 2026 at 11:11
> To: ietf@kenkubota.de <ietf@kenkubota.de>
> Cc: ietf@ietf.org <ietf@ietf.org>; tls@ietf.org <tls@ietf.org>; =
ietf-discussion@listi.jpberlin.de <ietf-discussion@listi.jpberlin.de>
> Subject: Re: [TLS] NSA / IETF Discussion List
>=20
> Dear Ken Kubota,
> IETF has SEAT working group. It works on TLS 1.3 extension. SEAT has =
https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-fossati-seat-early-attestation/.
> 41 high class CVEs were filed against it: =
https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-intra-handshake-fail/
> They also provided patch to fix. The patch was never applied.
> Anyone who talks about CVEs is given "official warning" by chairs.
> Is it in scope of your mailing list =
https://listi.jpberlin.de/mailman/listinfo/ietf-discussion?
> Thanks,
> Waqas
>=20
>=20
> On Wed, Oct 14, 2026 at 12:14 PM Ken Kubota <ietf@kenkubota.de> wrote:
> For those who disagree with NSA activities at the IETF or wish to find =
solutions for the issues concerning the current IETF situation, you can =
join this mailing list:
>=20
> https://listi.jpberlin.de/mailman/listinfo/ietf-discussion
>=20
>=20
> Kind regards,
>=20
> Ken Kubota
>=20
> ____________________________________________________
>=20
> Ken Kubota
> https://doi.org/10.4444/100
